# LINE ENDINGS ARE PINNED TO LF IN THE WORKING TREE, ON EVERY PLATFORM.
#
# This is not a style preference, and it is not about tidiness. Two checks in
# `npm test` compare a file on disk against a string this program generates,
# byte for byte:
#
#     docs/index.html   vs  renderSite(...)        `onDisk === expected`
#     README.md blocks  vs  the derived renderers
#
# Those strings are built from template literals, so every newline in them is
# `\n`. Git on Windows defaults to core.autocrlf=true, which rewrites LF to
# CRLF when it puts a file into the working tree. Nothing about the page would
# be wrong - every figure would still be correct - but every line would differ
# by one invisible byte and the comparison would fail.
#
# The failure would also lie about its cause. The message reads:
#
#     docs/index.html is stale - a figure on the landing page disagrees
#     with docs/*.json
#     fix: npm run build:site
#
# which would send someone hunting through precision figures for a wrong
# number that is not wrong, and `npm run build:site` would not fix it, because
# the regenerated file gets converted on the next checkout too.
#
# This has not bitten yet only because the files currently on disk were written
# there directly rather than checked out. A fresh clone, a `git checkout`, a
# second machine or the first outside contributor would all trigger it.
#
# `eol=lf` makes the working tree match the repository on every platform, so
# the generated file and the generator can never disagree over a newline.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The mark is binary. Never let any conversion near it.
*.png binary
*.woff2 binary
*.wasm binary
